Given the roots of two binary trees p and q, write a function to check if they are the same or not.

Two binary trees are considered the same if they are structurally identical, and the nodes have the same value.

 Our recursive approach
For every pair of nodes:
1. Check if both are null → true.
2. Check if only one is null → false.
3. Compare their values.
4. Recursively compare their left children.
5. Recursively compare their right children.

We don't need separate base conditions for p.left and p.right.

Why? Because every recursive call checks whether its current nodes are null.

class Solution {
    public boolean isSameTree(TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {

        // Case 1: Both nodes are null
        if (p == null && q == null) {
            return true;
        }

        // Case 2: Only one node is null
        if (p == null || q == null) {
            return false;
        }

        // Case 3: Node values are different
        if (p.val != q.val) {
            return false;
        }

        // Case 4: Compare left and right subtrees
        return isSameTree(p.left, q.left) &&
               isSameTree(p.right, q.right);
    }
}
****************************
We call both recursive functions in the same return because both the left subtree AND the right subtree must be identical.
       1                 1
      / \               / \
     2   3             2   3
    /                   /
   4                   5


At the root:
return isSameTree(p.left, q.left) &&
       isSameTree(p.right, q.right);


- First call checks the entire left subtree.
  - Node 2 matches.
  - Node 4 != Node 5.
  - Returns false.
- Second call checks the entire right subtree.
  - Node 3 matches.
  - Returns true.
Therefore:
return false && true;
